GIT CHEAT SHEET - llm_engineering
==================================

Remotes
  origin    = my fork        (tjtoed/llm_engineering)
  upstream  = Ed's repo      (ed-donner/llm_engineering)


1. BEFORE EDITING A LAB: make my own copy
-----------------------------------------
Never edit Ed's original notebook. Copy it first so his files merge cleanly later.
Keep the copy in the SAME folder (the notebooks use relative paths like ../assets).

  cp week4/day3.ipynb week4/day3_mine.ipynb

Already edited the original by accident? Copy it, then reset the original:

  cp week4/day3.ipynb week4/day3_mine.ipynb      # keep my changes
  git restore week4/day3.ipynb                   # put Ed's original back
                                                 # (discards uncommitted edits - copy FIRST)


2. AFTER A LAB: save my work
----------------------------
  git status                                     # see what changed
  git add week4/day3_mine.ipynb                  # stage specific files (avoid `git add .`)
  git commit -m "week4 day3: litellm + openrouter"
  git push                                       # send it to my fork on GitHub

Notebook tips:
  - Outputs are saved in the file. They can contain system info and the first
    characters of API keys. Use "Clear All Outputs" in VS Code first if unsure.
  - Stage by name so stray files stay out of commits.
  - week4/main.cpp and week4/main are already in .gitignore, so they won't show up.


3. BEFORE NEW MATERIAL: pull Ed's updates
-----------------------------------------
Commit my own work first so I start from a clean tree.

  git status                                     # should say "nothing to commit"
  git fetch upstream                             # download Ed's changes (touches nothing)
  git merge upstream/main                        # combine them with mine
  git push                                       # update my fork
  uv sync                                        # in case dependencies changed

Want to see what Ed changed before merging?

  git log HEAD..upstream/main --oneline          # his new commits
  git diff HEAD...upstream/main --stat           # files he changed


4. IF A MERGE GOES WRONG
------------------------
Conflict = we both changed the same file. Nothing is lost.

  git status                                     # lists "both modified" files
  git merge --abort                              # back out completely, as if I never merged

To resolve instead: open the file, pick which side to keep (VS Code shows
"Accept Current / Incoming / Both"), then:

  git add <file>
  git commit                                     # finishes the merge


5. HANDY EVERYDAY COMMANDS
--------------------------
  git status                                     # what's changed right now
  git diff                                       # exact changes not yet staged
  git log --oneline -10                          # recent commits
  git restore <file>                             # throw away uncommitted edits to a file
  git restore --staged <file>                    # un-stage a file (keeps my edits)
  git commit --amend -m "better message"         # fix the LAST commit message (before pushing)
  git stash                                      # park uncommitted changes temporarily
  git stash pop                                  # bring them back


6. API KEYS
-----------
Keys live in .env, which is git-ignored. Never paste keys into a notebook or commit them.
Check before committing:

  git diff --cached | grep -i "sk-"              # staged changes: anything key-like?
